  "content": {
    "id": "p20-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p20-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let $a=\\sqrt[3]{2}$. Find a polynomial with integer coefficients for which $a$ is a root.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=x^3-2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a^3&=2\\\\a^3-2&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $P(x)=x^3-2$ has integer coefficients and $P(a)=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p20-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ is irrational.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\sqrt[3]{2}$ is irrational.\n\nBy the Integer Root Theorem, the only possible rational roots of $x^3-2$ are $\\pm1,\\pm2$; none is a root.",
          "worked": "$P(x)=x^3-2$ is monic with integer coefficients. By the Integer Root Theorem, any rational root must be an integer factor of $2$, so the only possibilities are $1,-1,2,-2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=1^3-2=-1\\ne0\\\\P(-1)&=(-1)^3-2=-3\\ne0\\\\P(2)&=2^3-2=6\\ne0\\\\P(-2)&=(-2)^3-2=-10\\ne0\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $P(x)$ has no rational roots. Since $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ is a root, $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ is irrational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      }
    ]
  },
